<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>destination</key>
	<string>export</string>
	<key>generateAppStoreInformation</key>
	<false/>
	<key>manageAppVersionAndBuildNumber</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- Same method string as the iOS ExportOptions.plist: `app-store-connect`
	     is what `xcodebuild -help` lists for Xcode 26 ("Additional options
	     include app-store (deprecated: use app-store-connect)"). The method
	     does not name a platform — the *archive* does. Point this file at the
	     native macOS archive and the same method produces the macOS product. -->
	<key>method</key>
	<string>app-store-connect</string>
	<!-- One entry per MAIN EXECUTABLE in the archive. Since the native
	     restructure (backlog item 16) the macOS archive contains exactly one:
	     TimeConverterMac, carrying the same bundle id as the iOS app.

	     Two things are deliberately absent, and both used to be here:

	     - dev.thisolivier.timeconverter.controls. The Control Center extension
	       is iOS-only — there is no Control Center on the Mac — so the native
	       Mac app does not embed it and it needs no macOS profile. One fewer
	       portal artifact than the Catalyst lane required.
	     - dev.thisolivier.timeconverter.menubar. The loadable macOS-SDK bundle
	       that gave Catalyst a menu bar no longer exists; a native target
	       reaches NSStatusItem and Carbon directly.

	     The profile named below is a **macOS** profile. A profile is
	     platform-scoped: the iOS "TimeConverter App Store" profile reports
	     platforms "visionOS, watchOS, and iOS" and xcodebuild refuses it for
	     platform macOS outright. Its portal profile type is now plain "Mac"
	     (Mac App Store), NOT "Mac Catalyst" — see
	     docs/testflight-setup-checklist.md "macOS train". Same App ID, same
	     bundle id, no `maccatalyst.` prefix (that prefix was a Catalyst
	     concept and retired with it). -->
	<key>provisioningProfiles</key>
	<dict>
		<key>dev.thisolivier.timeconverter</key>
		<string>TimeConverter Mac App Store</string>
	</dict>
	<key>signingCertificate</key>
	<string>Apple Distribution</string>
	<key>signingStyle</key>
	<string>manual</string>
	<key>stripSwiftSymbols</key>
	<true/>
	<key>teamID</key>
	<string>CNHB9B5GX6</string>
	<key>testFlightInternalTestingOnly</key>
	<false/>
	<key>uploadSymbols</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- The macOS App Store product is an *installer package*, not an app
	     bundle: `man 7 altool` says "To upload your app binary files (.ipa or
	     .pkg)", Xcode Help's "Archive export files" says "for macOS apps, this
	     is an installer package (with a .pkg file extension)", and DTS is
	     blunt about it — "A Mac App Store app must be submitted as an installer
	     package" (forums 701581). So this export signs a .pkg, and a .pkg is
	     signed by an *installer* identity, which is a different certificate
	     type from a code-signing one: "Installer certificates have different
	     OIDs than code signing certificates. They are not interchangeable in
	     any way" (DTS, forums 740423).
	     `signingCertificate` above therefore does NOT cover it.

	     This requirement is unchanged by the native restructure: it is a
	     property of the macOS App Store, not of Catalyst. It is still the one
	     brand-new certificate the macOS train needs, and this machine does not
	     have it yet: `security find-identity -v` shows only Apple Distribution
	     and Apple Development, and under `-p macappstore` both report
	     "Missing required extension".
	     "Mac Installer Distribution" is an automatic selector — Xcode picks the
	     newest installed certificate of that type — and is also what this key
	     would default to; it is spelled out because being told
	     "No signing certificate 'Mac Installer Distribution' found" by
	     `-exportArchive` is otherwise mystifying. `scripts/ship-mac.sh`
	     preflights for it so the failure arrives before the archive, not after. -->
	<key>installerSigningCertificate</key>
	<string>Mac Installer Distribution</string>
</dict>
</plist>
